{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfhon9Amnw2bKiS6Kh5ZC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate, and it passes the admission self-check (`OK`). The check doesn't test novelty against the other models, and its short single-chain fit couldn't verify convergence. So admission's full fit and novelty gate could still reject it.\n\n**Model: `mismatch_noise_paired_comparison`** (this round's exploratory slot, which asked for a comparison-driven mechanism)\n\n- **Hypothesis:** people compare the two sequences flip against flip. Positions where both sequences show the same outcome cancel out, and only the positions where they differ carry evidence, and noise, into the choice. So how sure someone is depends on the partner: the same gap in randomness is judged decisively beside a near-identical sequence and hesitantly beside a very different one.\n- **How the model works:**\n  - Each sequence is scored the way the current best model scores it: how much better a fair coin believed to over-alternate explains it than a \"regular\" generator (a coin that tends to switch or repeat, or a biased coin).\n  - The new part is the comparison step. The score difference is divided by `sqrt(1 + γ·d)`, where `d` is the number of positions at which the two sequences differ and `γ` is a fitted parameter.\n  - Each person has their own sensitivity, as in the current best model.\n- **Why this mechanism:** short pairs can differ in only a few positions, so the model predicts people are more decisive on them. That targets the critique's finding, which held up after the multiple-comparison correction, that people are more decisive on short pairs than the best model predicts.\n- **Smoke fit:** the 100-draw ELPD-LOO came out at −1020.1, against −1021.6 for the current best model. Treat that as a rough sign only, since it came from a single short chain.\n- **Warning:** the smoke fit flagged 7.2% of trials as havin